.TH PATHMUX 1 "March 2026" "v1.0.0" "PathMux Dashcam Explorer Manual"
.SH NAME
pathmux \- Trip manager and video builder for multi-channel dashcam footage
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pathmux
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIPATH\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pathmux
scans dashcam footage directories, groups video segments into trips based on
timestamp gaps, and caches the results as JSON manifests.  Trips can be browsed
interactively, exported as GPX or KML tracks, and assembled into per-camera MP4
files or a synchronized 4K collage.
.PP
PathMux uses camera profiles to support different storage layouts.  The
built-in profile targets the Pruveeo D90 360\[de] four-channel dashcam
(Front, Rear, Left, Right subdirectories; MPEG-TS container; LIGO GPS stream).
Additional profiles for other cameras can be created interactively with
.BR "pm_probe \-\-wizard" .
.PP
The primary camera is the anchor for trip detection; secondary cameras are
fuzzy-matched within a configurable time window.  Segment timestamps come from
the filename (cameras that encode date and time in the filename) or from file
metadata via ExifTool (cameras that use a sequential counter instead of a
timestamp in the filename).  All timestamp comparisons use Unix epoch arithmetic
to handle midnight and year boundaries correctly.
.PP
Trip manifests are written to the scanned footage directory as
.IR pm_manifest_<id>.json .
If that directory is not writable, manifests fall back to
.IR ~/.config/pathmux/ .
Each scanned path is assigned a two-character alphanumeric manifest ID from an
unambiguous character set (ambiguous pairs such as O/0 and I/1 are collapsed).
IDs are reused when manifests are deleted, keeping the list compact.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Scan & Display"
.TP
.BI \-s " PATH" ", " \-\-scan " PATH"
Scan
.I PATH
for dashcam footage and build a trip manifest.  If a manifest already exists
for this path, prompts with three choices: overwrite it, delete it and rescan
from scratch, or quit.  Prints a one-line summary per trip on completion.
.TP
.B \-t
Print a compact one-line summary for each cached manifest, sorted by most
recent trip date.  Shows manifest ID, footage path, trip count, and date range.
Non-interactive; exits after printing.
.TP
.B \-T ", " \-\-dump
Print a multi-line block per manifest listing every trip: date, start time,
duration, and segment count.  No segment file paths are shown.
Non-interactive; exits after printing.
.TP
.B \-\-fulldump
Like
.B \-T
but includes all segment file paths for every trip and every camera.
.TP
.B \-\-jsondump
Dump all manifest and trip data as a single JSON document to stdout.
Segment paths are relative to each manifest's source path.
Suitable for piping to
.BR jq (1)
or scripting.
.TP
.BI \-T\ \-\-format= fmt
Structured output modifier.  Used together with
.BR \-T .
Valid values for
.I fmt
are
.BR json ,
.BR csv ,
and
.BR xml .
.RS
.TP
.B json
Equivalent to
.BR \-\-jsondump ;
the
.B \-\-fields
option is ignored.
.TP
.B csv
One record per trip, comma-separated.  A header row is emitted first.
Use
.B \-\-fields
to select which columns appear.  Default (no
.BR \-\-fields )
includes all available fields.
.TP
.B xml
One
.B <trip>
element per trip inside a
.B <trips>
root.  Use
.B \-\-fields
to select which child elements are emitted.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-fields= f1,f2,...
Comma-separated list of fields to include in
.B csv
or
.B xml
output.  Ignored for
.BR json .
Available field names:
.RS
.TP
.B manifest_id\fR,\fB trip_id
Manifest and trip identifiers.
.TP
.B address\fR,\fB date\fR,\fB start_time\fR,\fB start_epoch
Human-readable address (if geocoded), calendar date, wall-clock start
time, and Unix epoch of trip start.
.TP
.B duration\fR,\fB duration_seconds
Trip length as a formatted string and as raw seconds.
.TP
.B segment_count\fR,\fB note
Number of video segments; optional free-text note attached to the trip.
.TP
.B start_lat\fR,\fB start_lon\fR,\fB end_lat\fR,\fB end_lon
Start and end coordinates in decimal degrees (requires GPS extraction).
.TP
.B distance_km\fR,\fB distance_mi
Great-circle distance between start and end coordinates.
.TP
.B gps_lock_seconds
Seconds elapsed from segment start until the first GPS fix with a
non-zero position.  Requires GPS extraction; zero if not yet run.
.TP
.B gps_track_status
One of
.BR none ,
.BR partial ,
or
.BR complete .
.RE
.TP
.B \-I ", " \-\-interactive
Unified interactive browser.  Opens at the manifest list home screen.
Select a manifest by ID to view its trips; select a trip by ID to view
details, build video, or export GPS data.
Press
.B Q
at any prompt to return to the previous level or exit.
.SS "GPS & Export"
.TP
.B \-G ", " \-\-gps
Launch the interactive GPS extraction and export menu.
.PP
.B Trip picker screen
.RS
Displays all trips for the selected manifest with GPS status (none /
start\[sl]end / complete).  Available commands:
.TP
.I TripID
Select a single trip by its two-character ID (case-insensitive).
.TP
.B A
Select all trips (bulk GPS extraction or export).
.TP
.B E
Toggle verbose ExifTool error output on\[sl]off.  Useful when GPS extraction
returns no data and you need to see the raw ExifTool stderr.
.TP
.B M
Return to the manifest selection screen.
.TP
.B Q
Quit to the calling screen or exit.
.RE
.PP
.B Action menu
.RS
After selecting a trip or [A]ll, the action menu offers:
.TP
.B G
Extract GPS data from footage into the manifest (no file output).
.TP
.B X
Export a GPX track file (extracts GPS first if not yet done).
.TP
.B K
Export a KML track file (extracts GPS first if not yet done).
.TP
.B J
Export a GeoJSON track file (RFC 7946 FeatureCollection).
.TP
.B Q
Return to the trip picker.
.RE
.PP
.B Output directory
.RS
For GPX, KML, and GeoJSON exports, PathMux defaults to the footage source
directory if it is writable.  If the source directory is not writable, a
three-choice prompt appears: use the global default export directory
(configured via
.BR \-P ),
enter a path manually, or return to the trip picker.
The prompt accepts a directory path or a full file path; if a full path
ending in
.IR .gpx ,
.IR .kml ,
or
.I .geojson
is entered, that filename is used directly.
.RE
.PP
Requires ExifTool (see
.BR "GPS EXTRACTION" ).
.SS "Settings"
.TP
.B \-P ", " \-\-prefs
Launch the interactive preferences editor.  Settings are saved to
.IR ~/.config/pathmux/pathmux.json .
.TP
.B \-\-hostprefs
Launch the host-specific preferences editor.  Settings here are saved to
.BI ~/.config/pathmux/pathmux_ hostname .json
and are applied as an overlay on top of the base preferences.  They
affect only the current machine.  Use this to configure encoder paths,
hardware acceleration, output directories, and log level per host when
the same footage library is shared across multiple machines.
Available settings: FFmpeg path, ExifTool path, ExifTool options,
default output directory, temp directory, log level, and encoder
settings.
.TP
.B \-\-encoderprefs
Launch the interactive encoder preferences editor.  Configure hardware
acceleration (VAAPI, NVENC, etc.) or fall back to software encoding.
.TP
.B \-\-kmlprefs
Launch the interactive KML visual preferences editor (track colors, line
width, pin icons, known location overlay).
.TP
.B \-\-locations
Manage known locations (Home, Work, etc.) used for KML proximity pins.
.TP
.BI \-\-set " key=value"
Set a preference non-interactively.  Currently supported keys:
.RS
.TP
.B gap=\fIN\fR
Trip gap threshold in seconds (minimum 30).
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-show-config
Dump raw configuration key/value pairs and exit.
Useful for verifying the active ffmpeg/ExifTool paths and encoder settings.
.SS "Manifest Management"
.TP
.B \-\-clear-cache
Interactive list; pick one manifest to delete by ID.  The list redisplays
after each deletion; enter
.B Q
to exit.  Settings and known locations are preserved.
.TP
.B \-\-clear-cache ALL
Show the full manifest list, prompt for
.B y/N
confirmation, then wipe all manifests and the manifest index.
.TP
.B \-\-clear-cache ALL \-\-force
Wipe all manifests without any prompt.  Suitable for scripted use.
.B \-\-force
may appear anywhere after
.BR \-\-clear-cache .
.TP
.B \-\-show-stale
Display the stale manifest archive
.RI ( manifests_stale.json ).
Stale entries are index entries whose manifest file is no longer present on
disk; they are automatically archived when PathMux detects them at startup.
.TP
.B \-\-clear-stale
Clear the stale archive after a
.B y/N
confirmation prompt.
.TP
.B \-\-clear-stale \-\-force
Clear the stale archive without prompting.
.TP
.B \-\-validate
Check all cached manifests for consistency problems: missing manifest files
and md5 checksum mismatches (indicating external modification).  Prints a
per-manifest status report.
Exits with code
.B 0
if all manifests are clean,
.B 1
if any problems are found.
Non-interactive; suitable for cron or scripted health checks.
.TP
.B \-v ", " \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.TP
.B \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH "VIDEO BUILD"
Video builds are launched from within the interactive browser
.RB ( \-I ).
Select a manifest, then a trip, then choose
.B [V] Build video
from the trip detail screen.  The build options menu allows toggling
per-camera output files, collage resolution, audio extract, output
format, and output directory.
.PP
.B Build actions:
.TP
.B GO
Run the configured build and return to the trip picker on completion.
.TP
.B GODONE
Run the configured build and exit the video builder on completion.
.TP
.B [T] Compute duration
Run ffprobe on every Front camera segment to produce an accurate
total trip duration.  Displayed as
.B "Computed duration"
in the menu header once computed; stored in the manifest for future sessions.
.PP
A build log entry is appended to
.I pm_buildlog.json
in the footage source directory after each successful build.  The log
captures the manifest ID, trip ID, output path, build timestamp, output
duration, which cameras were included, and the full segment list consumed.
.SH "VIDEO BUILD PIPELINE"
Per-camera files use the ffmpeg concat demuxer with stream copy
.RB ( "\-c copy" ).
Only the video and audio streams are mapped
.RB ( "\-map 0:v:0 \-map 0:a:0" );
the LIGOGPSINFO data track embedded in the MPEG-TS container is discarded.
GPS data is already in the manifest at this point and does not need to travel
with the video file.
.PP
The 4K collage is assembled as follows:
.IP 1. 4
Per segment, ffprobe measures the frame count of all four cameras.
.IP 2. 4
Cameras shorter than the longest are padded with a fade-to-black tail
.RB ( tpad " + " fade " filters) and silent audio (" apad ).
This prevents sync drift from accumulating across a long trip.
.IP 3. 4
The four normalized streams are combined with the
.B xstack
filter into a 3840x2160 frame:
.PP
.RS
.nf
[ Front ] [ Rear  ]
[ Right ] [ Left  ]
.fi
.RE
.IP 4. 4
Audio is taken from the configured collage audio source (default: Left camera,
the driver position in left-hand-drive countries; change via preferences or
the
.B [H]
option in the build menu).
.IP 5. 4
Output is encoded as H.265
.RB ( "libx265 \-crf 18 \-preset slow" )
by default.  Hardware acceleration profiles (VAAPI, NVENC) can be configured
via
.BR \-\-encoderprefs .
.PP
The optional 1080p collage is a simple transcode of the finished 4K master
.RB ( "scale=1920:1080" ", " "libx264 \-crf 20 \-preset fast" )
with the audio stream copied without re-encoding.
.SH "GPS EXTRACTION"
GPS extraction uses ExifTool to read GPS metadata from footage segments.
PathMux passes the configured
.B exiftoolOptions
string to the ExifTool binary and parses the output; it does not validate
the ExifTool version.
If GPS extraction returns no data or corrupted/garbled data, verify that
ExifTool is installed and that the options string matches your camera's
GPS format.
If your camera's GPS format is not supported by ExifTool, contact the
ExifTool maintainer directly \(em PathMux does not maintain a list of
supported cameras or ExifTool version requirements.
.PP
One GPS fix per second is available.  During the initial trip scan, the
extractor reads the first Front camera segment to find the first fix with
a non-zero latitude and longitude, avoiding the cold-start window without
wasting a fixed offset on trips where the GPS receiver achieves a hot start
immediately.  The same pass stores the last valid fix from the final segment
as the trip end coordinate.
.PP
Altitude values from the D90 are systematically incorrect (negative) and are
ignored.  Speed is recorded in km/h; the dashcam OSD converts to mph for
display but the raw stream is metric.
.SS "ExifTool Options Preference"
The complete exiftool command line (excluding the binary path and the input
filename) is stored in the
.B exiftoolOptions
preference, editable via
.B \-\-prefs
option
.BR [O] .
The default value for Pruveeo D90 footage is:
.PP
.RS
.B \-ee3 \-p '$GPSDateTime $GPSLatitude# $GPSLongitude# $GPSAltitude# $GPSSpeed# $GPSTrack# $Accelerometer'
.RE
.PP
This string is passed verbatim between the binary path and the quoted filename.
The assembled command is:
.PP
.RS
.RI exiftool " OPTIONS" " \(dqfile.ts\(dq"
.RE
.PP
.B Important constraints when modifying this value:
.TP
.B Field order is fixed.
The output parser expects exactly seven space-separated tokens per line after
the date and time:
.IR latitude ,
.IR longitude ,
.IR altitude ,
.IR speed ,
.IR heading ,
.IR accelerometer .
Do not reorder, add, or remove fields from the
.B \-p
format string without corresponding changes to the parser source.
.TP
.B The \-p flag and format string must be included.
Unlike most tool preferences which store only extraction flags,
.B exiftoolOptions
must contain the complete options string including
.BR \-p .
This design allows the
.B \-p
flag itself to be omitted entirely for cameras that require a different
extraction approach (e.g.\&
.BR \-json ).
.TP
.B Single quotes are required around the format string.
The
.B \-p
argument must be quoted because it contains spaces and
.B $
characters.
Single quotes prevent the shell from expanding
.B $GPSDateTime
etc. as variables.
Double quotes would also expand
.BR $GPSTrack ,
corrupting the command.
When editing in the preferences menu, enter the value exactly as it would
appear on a shell command line.
.TP
.B The \-ee3 flag controls embedded stream extraction depth.
Level 3 is required for MPEG-TS files from the D90 because the GPS data
is in a private binary stream (LIGOGPSINFO) several levels deep in the
container.  Level 1
.RB ( \-ee )
and level 2
.RB ( \-ee2 )
do not reach it.
Other camera formats may work at lower levels or may not need
.B \-ee
at all.
.TP
.B The \-# numeric suffix on coordinate tags requests decimal degrees.
.B $GPSLatitude#
and
.B $GPSLongitude#
output signed decimal values (e.g.\&
.IR \-89.025 )
rather than the default DMS notation
.RI ( "89 deg 1' 30\(dq W" ).
The parser requires decimal form; remove the
.B #
suffix only if replacing the parser entirely.
.SH "MANIFEST IDS"
Manifests are assigned two-character IDs from an unambiguous alphanumeric
character set.  The following characters are excluded to avoid visual
confusion:
.PP
.RS
.B O
(same as
.BR 0 ),
.B I
and
.B L
(same as
.BR 1 ),
.B S
(same as
.BR 5 ),
.B Z
(same as
.BR 2 )
.RE
.PP
IDs are always displayed in upper case.  Input is case-insensitive.
The preferred sequence assigns a letter first character and digit second
character (A3, A4 ... Y9) giving 132 slots before spilling into
letter+letter combinations.  Deleted manifest IDs are returned to the pool
and reused from the lowest available position.
.PP
Trips within a manifest are also assigned two-character base36 IDs.
A specific trip can be referenced as
.IR MID : TID
(e.g.\&
.BR CQ:73 )
in tool output and future batch operations.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/pathmux/profiles/<name>.json
Camera profiles created by
.BR "pm_probe \-\-wizard" .
The active profile is selected by setting
.B activeProfileId
in preferences.
.TP
.I ~/.config/pathmux/pathmux.json
Application settings (base preferences, all hosts).
.TP
.BI ~/.config/pathmux/pathmux_ hostname .json
Host-specific settings overlay written by
.BR \-\-hostprefs .
Merged on top of the base preferences at startup; only the current
machine's file is loaded.
.TP
.I ~/.config/pathmux/manifests.json
Manifest index — maps each scanned footage path to its two-character ID.
.TP
.I <scanned_path>/pm_manifest_<id>.json
Cached trip manifest written to the scanned directory.  This is the primary
location; the path is only written to
.I ~/.config/pathmux/
when the scanned directory is not writable.
.TP
.I ~/.config/pathmux/manifests_stale.json
Archive of stale manifest index entries — paths whose manifest file was not
found on disk at startup validation.  Written automatically; never read during
normal operations.  Display with
.BR \-\-show-stale ;
wipe with
.BR \-\-clear-stale .
.TP
.I ~/.config/pathmux/locations.json
Named locations (Home, Work, etc.) used for KML proximity pin overlays.
.TP
.I <scanned_path>/pm_buildlog.json
Build provenance log appended after each successful video build.  Each entry
records the build timestamp, manifest ID, trip ID, output path and filename,
output duration in seconds, which cameras were included, and the full list of
source segments consumed.  Entries are never removed by PathMux; the file
grows over time as builds accumulate.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Failure (bad arguments, missing path, or ffmpeg/ExifTool error).
.B \-\-validate
exits 1 if any manifest problems are found.
.SH "BRAG BOARD"
Community-submitted 4K collage encode timings.
.PP
.B Score\ =\ footage_minutes\ /\ collage_4k_minutes
(higher is better).
.PP
.B "Submission requirements:"
.br
Daytime trip only \(em minimum 20 minutes of footage \(em all four cameras
(Front/Rear/Left/Right) included \(em 4K collage build.
.PP
.B "1.  Nutball Labs"
.br
i7 / 16\ GB\ DDR5 / RTX\ 5060 / NVMe
.br
42m\ trip\ \(em\ concat\ (4\ cameras):\ 4m\ 15s\ \(em\ 4K\ collage\ (hevc_nvenc):\ 6m\ 58s\ \(em\ score:\ \fB6.04x\fR
.PP
Want your name here?
After a qualifying build, find your timings in
.I pm_buildlog.json
in your footage source directory (or
.I $APPDATA/pathmux/pm_buildlog.json
on Windows if the source path was not writable).
Submit via a
.I "Brag Board submission"
issue on the PathMux GitHub tracker.
